      "Above the selected teaching window, pressure on a wet stack raises separator-stress and particle-driven puncture risk. Actual limits depend on separator, stack geometry, cleanliness and equipment validation."]
    : m.wetting >= 96 ? ["ok", "Electrolyte distributed",
      `${m.wetting.toFixed(0)}% illustrative wetting. More uniform electrolyte distribution supports more uniform formation, but this score does not prove that every pore is wetted.`]
    : ["warn", "Still short of full wetting",
      "Raise the pressure a little or leave the cell longer before formation."],
  viz:m => ({ press: clamp(S.pressP / 1.2 * 100) })
},

formation: {
  lead:"You are at formation — one of the most capital- and time-intensive stages in the reference line. The first controlled cycles form the SEI; rate, temperature, voltage limits, pressure and cleanliness all influence the result.",
  ctl:["cRate","formT","vMax"],
  read:m => [
    V("First charge", S.cRate.toFixed(2) + " C", ok(S.cRate, 0.1, 0.5), "PEM: 0.1–0.5 C"),
    V("Illustrative SEI score", m.seiQ.toFixed(0) + "%", m.seiQ >= 90 ? "ok" : m.seiQ >= 80 ? "warn" : "bad"),
    V("Illustrative irreversible loss", m.liLoss.toFixed(1) + "%", m.liLoss < 12 ? "ok" : "warn", "teaching estimate of first-cycle cyclable-lithium consumption"),
    V("Time on the rack", (13 / (S.cRate / 0.25)).toFixed(1) + " h", (13 / (S.cRate / 0.25)) <= 15 ? "ok" : "warn", "PEM: up to 15 h"),
    V("Cell temperature", S.formT + " °C", band(S.formT, 20, 50, 8)),
    V("Upper cut-off", S.vMax.toFixed(2) + " V", S.vMax <= 4.25 ? "ok" : "bad")],
  verdict:m => S.cRate > 0.42 ? ["bad", "Charging too fast",
      "The selected first-charge rate is near the upper end of the reference range. Faster formation can change interphase properties and raise plating risk under some conditions; validated recipes are chemistry- and design-specific."]
    : S.vMax > 4.3 ? ["bad", "Cut-off too high",
      "The selected cut-off exceeds the reference graphite/NMC teaching limit. Electrolyte oxidation, gas generation and accelerated degradation risk can rise, but actual limits depend on materials and protocol."]
    : m.seiQ >= 90 ? ["ok", "SEI forming well",
      `${m.seiQ.toFixed(0)}% illustrative SEI score and ${m.liLoss.toFixed(1)}% illustrative first-cycle irreversible loss. Actual values depend on materials, electrolyte and formation protocol.`]
    : ["warn", "SEI quality mediocre",
      "Review the first-charge rate and the validated formation-temperature window for this chemistry and cell design."],
  viz:m => ({ sei: m.seiQ, rate: clamp((S.cRate - 0.1) / 0.4 * 100) })
},

degassing: {
  lead:"You are at degassing — pouch cells only. Formation made gas inside a bag that cannot vent, so you cut the pocket open, pull the gas out and re-seal.",
  ctl:["degP","cRate"],
  read:m => [
    V("Vacuum level", S.degP + " mbar", S.degP <= 10 ? "ok" : "warn"),
    V("Gas remaining", m.gasLeft.toFixed(1) + "%", m.gasLeft < 6 ? "ok" : m.gasLeft < 12 ? "warn" : "bad"),
    V("Gas generated at formation", (S.cRate * 34).toFixed(0) + " (index)", S.cRate <= 0.35 ? "ok" : "warn", "gentler formation makes less gas"),
    V("Final seal", "made after evacuation", "ok"),
    V("Cell thickness after", m.gasLeft < 6 ? "nominal" : "swollen", m.gasLeft < 6 ? "ok" : "warn")],
  verdict:m => m.gasLeft > 12 ? ["bad", "Gas left in the cell",
      "A pouch with trapped gas has layers that are not touching. Those areas do not work, and the cell swells further in service."]
    : ["ok", "Degassed and re-sealed",
      `${m.gasLeft.toFixed(1)}% residual gas at ${S.degP} mbar. Hard-case gas-management and closure sequences vary by design; pressure-relief devices do not replace controlled formation-gas management.`],
  viz:m => ({ gas: clamp(100 - m.gasLeft * 6) })
},

aging: {
  lead:"You are at aging. The cell is rested and monitored for OCV drift and other release indicators. This screen can reveal elevated self-discharge or latent internal defects, but it is not a standalone proof of cell safety.",
  ctl:["ageDays","ageHiT","ageSOC"],
  read:m => [
    V("Total period", S.ageDays + " d", ok(S.ageDays, 3, 21), "PEM: up to 3 weeks"),
    V("High-temperature stage", S.ageHiT + " °C", ok(S.ageHiT, 30, 50), "PEM: 30–50 °C"),
    V("State of charge", S.ageSOC + "%", ok(S.ageSOC, 30, 80), "PEM: 30–80%"),
    V("Screen sensitivity", m.screenSens.toFixed(0) + "%", m.screenSens >= 88 ? "ok" : m.screenSens >= 72 ? "warn" : "bad"),
    V("Defects that would escape", (Math.max(0, 100 - m.screenSens) * 0.06).toFixed(2) + "%", m.screenSens >= 88 ? "ok" : "warn"),
    V("Floor space cost", (S.ageDays * 3.6).toFixed(0) + " (index)", S.ageDays <= 16 ? "ok" : "warn", "weeks of inventory standing still")],
  verdict:m => m.screenSens < 72 ? ["bad", "Screen too short to be trusted",
      "A slow self-discharge fault needs time and controlled temperature to appear as measurable voltage drift. Shortening the screen can reduce sensitivity."]
    : S.ageDays > 18 ? ["warn", "Very long — this is inventory",
      "Every extra day is finished cells sitting in a heated warehouse. Aging and formation together dominate cell-finishing cost."]
    : ["ok", "Screen adequate",
      `${S.ageDays} days, ${S.ageHiT} °C stage, ${m.screenSens.toFixed(0)}% illustrative sensitivity using a 5 mV/week teaching flag.`],
  viz:m => ({ screen: m.screenSens })
},

eol: {
  lead:"You are at end of line. Run the defined performance tests, grade the cell, set the required shipping SOC, verify the applicable transport instruction, label and pack it.",
  ctl:["shipSOC","ageDays"],
  read:m => [
    V("Reference capacity estimate", m.cellAh.toFixed(1) + " Ah", "ok", "the actual capacity test requires a defined charge/rest/discharge protocol"),
    V("Example plant target", S.shipSOC + "% SOC", band(S.shipSOC, 10, 20, 10), "illustrative target; manufacturer-specific"),
    V("PI 965 air-shipping check", m.shipOK ? "≤30% — pass" : ">30% — fail", m.shipOK ? "ok" : "bad", "UN 3480 cells/batteries shipped alone by air; other shipment configurations use different instructions"),
    V("Process-health score", m.yieldPct.toFixed(1) + "%", m.yieldPct >= 95 ? "ok" : m.yieldPct >= 90 ? "warn" : "bad"),
    V("Cells per day, this line", fmt(m.cellsDay), "ok"),
    V("Annual output, this line", m.gwh.toFixed(1) + " GWh", "ok"),
    V("Grading spread", "±" + (m.coatErr * 1.4).toFixed(1) + "% capacity", m.coatErr <= 2 ? "ok" : "warn", "coating scatter arrives here")],
  verdict:m => !m.shipOK ? ["bad", "Above the air-freight limit",
      "For this UN 3480 shipped-alone air scenario, PI 965 limits state of charge to 30%. Other shipment configurations and transport modes use different requirements."]
    : m.yieldPct < 90 ? ["warn", "Illustrative process-health score is low",
      "Walk back up the line — the advice panel names the worst problem, and it is almost always upstream of here."]
    : ["ok", "Cells graded and released",
      `${m.cellAh.toFixed(1)} Ah at ${m.yieldPct.toFixed(1)}% process-health score, packed at ${S.shipSOC}% SOC.`],
  viz:m => ({ grade: m.yieldPct })
}
};
</script>
<script>
/* =====================================================================
   RESPONSE CHARTS
   Every curve below is produced by actually re-running the process model
   across a setpoint's whole range — it is not a drawing. Move the knob and
   the dot moves along the same curve the model just computed.
   ===================================================================== */

function sweep(key, get, n = 60) {
  const c = CTL[key], old = S[key], out = [];
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
    const v = c.min + (c.max - c.min) * i / n;
    S[key] = c.step >= 1 ? Math.round(v) : v;
    out.push([S[key], get(model())]);
  }
  S[key] = old;
  return out;
}

const W = 340, HH = 168, PL = 46, PR = 12, PT = 22, PB = 30;

function chartSVG(cf) {
  const c = CTL[cf.x];
  const runs = cf.series.map(s => sweep(cf.x, s.y));
  const all = runs.flat().map(p => p[1]).filter(v => isFinite(v));
  let lo = cf.lo != null ? cf.lo : Math.min(...all);
  let hi = cf.hi != null ? cf.hi : Math.max(...all);
  if (cf.good) { lo = Math.min(lo, cf.good[0]); hi = Math.max(hi, cf.good[1]); }
  if (cf.limit != null) { lo = Math.min(lo, cf.limit); hi = Math.max(hi, cf.limit); }
  if (hi - lo < 1e-9) hi = lo + 1;
  const pad = (hi - lo) * 0.1; lo -= pad; hi += pad;

  const px = v => PL + (v - c.min) / (c.max - c.min) * (W - PL - PR);
  const py = v => PT + (1 - (Math.max(lo, Math.min(hi, v)) - lo) / (hi - lo)) * (HH - PT - PB);
  const dp = cf.dp != null ? cf.dp : 0;

  /* the band of outcomes you are allowed to land in */
  let band = "";
  if (cf.good) {
    const y1 = py(Math.min(cf.good[1], hi)), y2 = py(Math.max(cf.good[0], lo));
    band = `<rect x="${PL}" y="${y1}" width="${W - PL - PR}" height="${Math.max(1, y2 - y1)}"
      fill="color-mix(in srgb,var(--primary) 10%,transparent)"/>
      <text class="cLab" x="${W - PR - 3}" y="${y1 + 11}" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--primary)">${esc(cf.goodLabel || "acceptable")}</text>`;
  }
  let limit = "";
  if (cf.limit != null) limit = `<line x1="${PL}" y1="${py(cf.limit)}" x2="${W - PR}" y2="${py(cf.limit)}"
      stroke="var(--destructive)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>
      <text class="cLab" x="${PL + 3}" y="${py(cf.limit) - 4}" fill="var(--destructive)">${esc(cf.limitLabel || "limit")}</text>`;
  /* a hard boundary on the setpoint itself, e.g. a regulatory ceiling */
  let xmark = "";
  if (cf.xMark != null) xmark = `<rect x="${px(cf.xMark)}" y="${PT}" width="${W - PR - px(cf.xMark)}"
      height="${HH - PT - PB}" fill="color-mix(in srgb,var(--destructive) 8%,transparent)"/>
      <line x1="${px(cf.xMark)}" y1="${PT}" x2="${px(cf.xMark)}" y2="${HH - PB}" stroke="var(--destructive)" stroke-width="1.4"/>
      <text class="cLab" x="${px(cf.xMark) + 4}" y="${PT + 10}" fill="var(--destructive)">${esc(cf.xMarkLabel || "not allowed")}</text>`;

  /* is an outcome value acceptable? drives the colour of the curve */
  const okAt = v => cf.good ? (v >= cf.good[0] && v <= cf.good[1])
    : cf.limit != null ? v <= cf.limit
    : true;
  const hasState = !!(cf.good || cf.limit != null);

  /* the curves — the primary one is drawn in two colours, so the part of
     the range that lands out of specification is red on the chart itself */
  const lines = runs.map((pts, i) => {
    const s = cf.series[i];
    if (i > 0 || !hasState) {
      const d = pts.map((p, j) => `${j ? "L" : "M"}${px(p[0]).toFixed(1)} ${py(p[1]).toFixed(1)}`).join("");
      return `<path d="${d}" fill="none" stroke="${s.col || "var(--primary)"}" stroke-width="2.2"
        stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round" ${s.dash ? 'stroke-dasharray="5 4"' : ""}/>`;
    }
    let dIn = "", dOut = "";
    for (let j = 1; j < pts.length; j++) {
      const a = pts[j - 1], b = pts[j];
      const seg = `M${px(a[0]).toFixed(1)} ${py(a[1]).toFixed(1)}L${px(b[0]).toFixed(1)} ${py(b[1]).toFixed(1)}`;
      if (okAt(a[1]) && okAt(b[1])) dIn += seg; else dOut += seg;
    }
    return `<path d="${dOut}" fill="none" stroke="var(--destructive)" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round"/>
      <path d="${dIn}" fill="none" stroke="${s.col || "var(--primary)"}" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"/>`;
  }).join("");

  /* where you are standing right now */
  const m = model(), xv = S[cf.x];
  const dots = cf.series.map((s, i) => {
    const v = s.y(m);
    const off = i === 0 && hasState && !okAt(v);
    return `<circle cx="${px(xv)}" cy="${py(v)}" r="${off ? 5.4 : 4.6}"
      fill="${off ? "var(--destructive)" : "#fff"}" stroke="${off ? "var(--destructive)" : (s.col || "var(--primary)")}" stroke-width="2.4"/>`;
  }).join("");
  const curOff = hasState && !okAt(cf.series[0].y(m));

  /* legend */
  const leg = cf.series.length > 1 ? cf.series.map((s, i) =>
    `<g transform="translate(${PL + i * 132} ${HH - 4})">
       <line x1="0" y1="-3" x2="13" y2="-3" stroke="${s.col}" stroke-width="2.2" ${s.dash ? 'stroke-dasharray="4 3"' : ""}/>
       <text class="cLab" x="17" y="0">${esc(s.label)}</text></g>`).join("") : "";

  const cur = cf.series[0].y(m);
  const xdp = c.step < 1 ? String(c.step).split(".")[1].length : 0;
  /* axis labels: the current-value tick wins, and any end label it would
     collide with is dropped rather than drawn on top of it */
  const tw = t => String(t).length * 5.0;                      /* ≈ width at 9 px */
  const loTxt = `${c.min}${c.unit || ""}`, hiTxt = `${c.max}${c.unit || ""}`;
  const nowTxt = xv.toFixed(xdp);
  const nowX = Math.max(PL + tw(nowTxt) / 2, Math.min(W - PR - tw(nowTxt) / 2, px(xv)));
  const nowL = nowX - tw(nowTxt) / 2 - 5, nowR = nowX + tw(nowTxt) / 2 + 5;
  const loLab = nowL > PL + tw(loTxt)
    ? `<text class="cAx" x="${PL}" y="${HH - PB + 13}">${esc(loTxt)}</text>` : "";
  const hiLab = nowR < W - PR - tw(hiTxt)
    ? `<text class="cAx" x="${W - PR}" y="${HH - PB + 13}" text-anchor="end">${esc(hiTxt)}</text>` : "";
  return `<figure class="chart" data-off="${curOff}">
    <figcaption><b>${esc(cf.title)}</b><span>${esc(c.label)} → ${esc(cf.series[0].label)}</span></figcaption>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${HH}" role="img" aria-label="${esc(cf.title)}: ${esc(c.label)} against ${esc(cf.series[0].label)}">
      <line x1="${PL}" y1="${PT}" x2="${PL}" y2="${HH - PB}" stroke="var(--border-strong)"/>
      <line x1="${PL}" y1="${HH - PB}" x2="${W - PR}" y2="${HH - PB}" stroke="var(--border-strong)"/>
      ${band}${xmark}${limit}${lines}
      <line x1="${px(xv)}" y1="${PT}" x2="${px(xv)}" y2="${HH - PB}" stroke="var(--foreground)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" opacity=".45"/>
      ${dots}
      ${loLab}${hiLab}
      <text class="cNow" x="${nowX}" y="${HH - PB + 13}" text-anchor="middle">${xv.toFixed(xdp)}</text>
      ${leg}
      <text class="cAx" x="${PL - 5}" y="${py(hi - pad) + 4}" text-anchor="end">${(hi - pad).toFixed(dp)}</text>
